    historyContent: {
      origins: "Dominoes originated in China in the 12th century (documented in the 'Customs and Traditions of Chu-chiang'). They were brought to Italy in the 18th century, likely by missionaries, where they were modified into the European double-six set.",
      originCountries: [
        'China',
        'Italy',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1120 AD: Earliest reliable records of dominoes in China.',
        '1700s: Dominoes appear in Italy (Venice and Naples).',
        '1800s: Spreads to France and England, becoming a staple of pub culture.',
        'Modern Era: Global popularity with high-level professional tournaments.',
      ],
      evolution: "Chinese dominoes represent all possible outcomes of throwing two dice. European dominoes added 'blank' ends to create the standard double-six (28 tile) set.",
      cultural: 'Used for both gaming and divination (especially in Asian cultures). In the West, it is a quintessential leisure game for all ages.',
